+# Accounts service FMEA
+
+Review basis: source and tests in this repository, the service README, and the operational assumptions in
+`AGENTS.md`, reviewed 4 November 2026. This is an FMEA of the current release candidate, so a row can describe a
+known implementation gap as well as an external failure.
+
+## Scoring
+
+Scores are on a 1–10 scale. Severity (S) is the effect if the failure reaches a customer or dependent process;
+occurrence (O) is the expected likelihood in production; detection (D) is the likelihood that existing controls fail
+to detect it before harm (10 means difficult to detect). RPN = S × O × D. Scores are relative prioritisation aids,
+not probabilities. Any row involving an unauthorised disclosure, duplicate debit, unscreened payment, or a payment
+sent without a corresponding ledger posting is release-blocking regardless of RPN.
+
+| Failure mode | Likely cause | Effect on customers and dependants | Detection / existing controls | S | O | D | RPN | Recommended action |
+|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| Payment is sent without fraud screening | `PaymentService.create` calls the dispatcher directly; `FraudScreeningClient` is not wired into the payment path | Unscreened payment may enable fraud or sanctions loss. Fraud team has no hold/review item; policy FRD-7 is breached and the bank may have to recall funds | Code review and a test spy on the fraud client would reveal zero calls; no production business metric currently proves every payment was screened | 10 | 8 | 9 | **720** | Block release. Screen every payment before scheme submission with the 2-second timeout. Persist the decision and audit event. `ACCEPT` continues, `REFER` becomes `HELD_FOR_REVIEW` without balance debit or scheme call, and `REJECT` becomes `REJECTED` with reason `FRAUD` only. |
+| Scheme accepts but the service reports failure and loses the payment | `SchemeTimeoutException` is not caught; the transaction rolls back, including the local payment row, although the scheme may have accepted `P` + payment id | Customer sees an error and may retry; the scheme can pay once or twice while the service has no record. Reconciliation and complaints investigations cannot reliably identify the payment | HTTP 5xx/error-rate alert may fire, but there is no durable payment or timeout queue to reconcile; `FakeScheme` explicitly tests accepted-before-timeout behaviour | 10 | 5 | 9 | **450** | Persist the payment before submission and move it to an explicit `SCHEME_UNKNOWN`/`SUBMISSION_PENDING` state on timeout. Query the scheme by the same `P` reference, then resolve to submitted/rejected; never create a new reference on retry. Add an exceptions report and alert on aged unknowns. |
+| Same instruction is posted to the ledger more than once | Retry or concurrent queue execution generates a fresh UUID in `dispatch` each time; the ledger idempotency reference is not stable | Customer can be debited twice after a timeout or duplicate job run. Finance reconciliation breaks and operations must remediate funds | Ledger-versus-scheme reconciliation at 07:00 can find a mismatch, but it is after the debit; no uniqueness constraint or stable-reference check prevents it | 10 | 5 | 8 | **400** | Generate and persist `ledgerReference` before the first post, reuse it for every retry, and make dispatch state transitions/row selection atomic. Add a database uniqueness constraint and a test for timeout-after-booking followed by retry. |
+| Payment is accepted by the scheme but ledger posting fails or times out | Ledger timeout is caught only as `POSTING_FAILED`; non-timeout HTTP errors are not translated, and the scheme and ledger are not one transaction | Payment may settle without a core-ledger debit; customer balance copy is reduced but the ledger is authoritative. Finance and operations must investigate; customer notification currently still uses `PAYMENT_SENT` | `POSTING_FAILED` status and log exist, plus daily exceptions report is an operational requirement; there is no automatic reconciliation or alert shown in code | 10 | 5 | 7 | **350** | Keep the durable stable ledger reference, classify all ledger failures, notify with `PAYMENT_FAILED` for an unresolved post, and expose an operations retry/reconciliation workflow. Alert on any `POSTING_FAILED` and reconcile scheme settlement to ledger by payment reference. |
+| Fraud service outage is treated as approval or the hold is bypassed | Client has fail-closed exception semantics, but it is not called by the flow; future integration could accidentally catch the error as a normal request failure | Unscreened funds could leave the bank, or customers may see inconsistent errors instead of a review hold; fraud team workload and regulatory evidence are incomplete | Fraud client timeout is configured at 2 seconds and a fake can simulate unavailability, but there is no end-to-end fail-closed test in the current payment tests | 10 | 4 | 8 | **320** | Add contract and API tests proving timeout/error always creates `HELD_FOR_REVIEW`, never calls scheme/ledger, does not debit, and creates the fraud-team work item. Monitor screening latency, errors, and the ratio of payments with a decision. |
+| Balance is stale or two payments race past available funds/daily limit | `accounts.balance` is a ledger copy; create checks and debit are not protected by a row lock/atomic reservation, and the service does not check the ledger for exact funds | Customer can be refused despite funds, or two concurrent payments can overspend and/or exceed the daily limit. Ledger, finance, complaints, and customer-visible balances diverge | Nightly ledger overwrite and daily reconciliation are the only stated controls; concurrent requests are not covered by the API tests | 9 | 6 | 8 | **432** | Treat the balance as a snapshot: reserve funds and enforce balance/daily-limit checks atomically per account, with pessimistic/optimistic locking. Reconcile reservations against ledger and surface stale-balance exceptions; use the ledger for any exact-balance decision. |
+| Payment identifiers are disclosed through the API | `PaymentView` returns `payeeSortCode` and `payeeAccountNumber` in both create and get responses; only notification formatting masks to last four digits | A full account number/sort code is exposed to customers or any authorised viewer, creating a reportable data-protection breach and increased payment-fraud risk | Static review can see the fields; access tests check owner visibility but do not assert masking. Logs/API gateway traces may retain the response | 9 | 4 | 8 | **288** | Remove full account identifiers from `PaymentView`; return only `accountEnding` (and, if needed, masked sort code). Add schema and regression tests that fail if 8-digit account numbers appear in API responses, notifications, or logs. |
+| A queued payment is sent on a bank holiday or rejected with `DT01` | `WorkingDays` knows weekdays only; bank holidays are not modelled, and the 06:00 job runs every Monday–Friday | Customer is told/assumes a payment will be sent on the next working day, but the scheme may reject it or it may miss the intended date. Operations and customer support handle avoidable rejects | Scheme rejection reason `DT01` is recorded; there is no bank-holiday calendar or alert on queued payments rejected for date | 8 | 6 | 7 | **336** | Use an authoritative UK bank-holiday calendar with versioned configuration, inject it into both queue-date calculation and job eligibility, and test year-end/holiday boundaries. Alert on overdue `QUEUED` items and `DT01` spikes. |
+| Queued payment is not dispatched, or is dispatched concurrently | Scheduler is single-process/assumed reliable; no claim/lock is used in `runDue`, and the job has no retry/dead-letter handling | Payment misses its promised date; duplicate workers can submit the same scheme instruction and, more seriously, create separate ledger references. Customers, operations, and the scheme receive inconsistent state | Job logs and the required overdue-queue report are potential detection points; no durable job-run metric, lock, or age SLO is present | 8 | 5 | 8 | **320** | Make due-payment claiming atomic (`QUEUED` → `DISPATCHING` with lease/owner), make processing resumable, and use stable scheme/ledger references. Add metrics for due, claimed, completed, failed, and overdue counts with alerts. |
+| Notification says a payment was sent when it was not fully posted, or no queue/hold message is sent | Dispatcher always calls `notifySent` after a scheme acceptance, including `POSTING_FAILED`; queued and fraud outcomes have no corresponding notification path | Customer may believe money left successfully when ledger posting needs remediation, or may not understand why a queued/held payment is delayed. Support complaints increase | Notification failures are caught and logged; there is no template/status consistency check or notification delivery metric | 7 | 6 | 7 | **294** | Map notifications to durable status transitions: queued → `PAYMENT_QUEUED`, held → `PAYMENT_UNDER_REVIEW`, completed → `PAYMENT_SENT`, unresolved posting → `PAYMENT_FAILED`. Keep notifications best effort, but add structured outcome metrics and an outbox if delivery evidence is required. |
+| Payment is submitted twice to the scheme after a lost response | Caller/job retries after a timeout without knowing whether the first instruction was accepted; current local transaction rollback can create a new payment id and therefore a new `P` reference | Payee may receive duplicate funds; customer is double-debited or has a duplicate settlement; reconciliation and complaints are difficult | Scheme reference uniqueness makes the same reference safe, but the current lost-row path prevents reuse; settlement report can detect duplicates only after the fact | 10 | 4 | 8 | **320** | Apply the same durable pre-submit record and status-query workflow as above. Enforce idempotency on the API request/customer reference where business-appropriate and never retry with a new payment id for the same instruction. |
+| Account is closed with money remaining or cannot be approved safely | Closure request creation does not check the core ledger, `ClosureController` exposes no approve/close operation, and `ClosureRequest` uses `Instant.now()` rather than the injected clock | Funds can be stranded in a closed account, or valid closures remain pending indefinitely. Approvers, operations, finance, and customers lack a reliable closure audit trail | Pending requests are visible to tenant approvers and account status is checked when requesting; there is no ledger-zero check or approval-path test | 10 | 3 | 9 | **270** | Implement the approval workflow with separation of requester/approver, re-check account state and exact ledger balance at approval/close time, make the close transition atomic, and use the injected clock for audit timestamps. Alert on aged pending requests. |
+| Tenant or resource access control leaks data | Access policy is mostly tenant-aware, but every new endpoint/query must preserve the 404-for-invisible-resource rule; payment view includes sensitive payee data | Cross-tenant disclosure or unauthorised payment/account access harms customers and creates regulatory/security incident response | JWT/resource tests cover several owner/support cases; security review and negative tests are needed for every endpoint, role combination, and tenant | 10 | 2 | 7 | **140** | Maintain deny-by-default endpoint rules and repository queries scoped by tenant/resource visibility. Add a matrix of cross-tenant, support-assignment, admin, expired-token, missing-scope, and payment-id tests; alert on 401/403/404 anomalies. |
+| Monthly statements are absent, late, or wrong | Repository/entity exist but no statement generation, retrieval endpoint, or scheduled cut is implemented in the service; the ledger is the source for closing balances | Customers may lack statements; regulatory return by the fifth working day can be incomplete or wrong. Finance and complaints teams cannot reproduce the customer record | Missing statement counts and regulator/finance reconciliation would detect it eventually; there is no in-service job, freshness metric, or completeness check | 9 | 5 | 9 | **405** | Implement an idempotent statement cut from ledger closing balances, including period completeness and corrections/audit trail. Add a per-tenant/account completeness report, freshness SLO, and alert well before the fifth working day. |
+| Account/customer data is inconsistent or unavailable | Database outage, failed migration/initialisation, H2-like assumptions in production, or partial writes across account/payment/case data | Customers cannot view balances or transact; support, complaints, finance, and reporting lose access or see incomplete history | Health endpoint and application/database logs exist; no readiness check, migration verification, backup-restore test, or SLO dashboard is represented in the repository | 9 | 3 | 6 | **162** | Add production-grade database migrations, readiness checks that validate required schema/data access, backups and restore drills, transaction/connection-pool metrics, and an availability/error-budget alert. Never use fixture initialisation as a production data-control. |
+| System clock/time-zone or cut-off calculation is wrong | Business rules require UK time, but some entities use `Instant.now()` directly and scheduler/configuration can drift from the injected clock; cut-off comparison is boundary-sensitive | Payment is sent late, delayed unnecessarily, or queued for the wrong date; statements/closure audit times and regulatory deadlines can be wrong | `Clock` is injectable in payment code and tests use `MutableClock`, but direct `now()` calls and boundary/holiday tests are not comprehensively guarded | 8 | 4 | 7 | **224** | Inject the clock everywhere, standardise on `Europe/London` business dates, test exactly 15:29:59/15:30/15:30:01, DST changes, weekends, and holidays, and monitor scheduler lateness. |
+| API error handling leaves an external side effect unknown | Only selected exceptions are mapped; scheme timeout and many `RestClient`/runtime errors can escape, while the surrounding transaction rolls back after an external call | Customer receives a generic 5xx and retries; local state may not describe a scheme/ledger side effect. Operations cannot distinguish safe retry from status query | HTTP 5xx metrics and integration logs may show the symptom; there is no correlation id, durable attempt record, or unknown-outcome queue | 9 | 5 | 8 | **360** | Define an explicit state machine for each external call, persist attempt/correlation data before the call, map errors by recoverability, and provide status-query/reconciliation workflows. Alert separately on unknown outcomes rather than treating them as ordinary 5xxs. |
+
+## Release priorities
+
+1. Block release until fraud screening is on the payment path and fails closed, and until scheme timeout handling preserves
+   the payment and reuses the same scheme reference.
+2. Make ledger references durable and idempotent across retries; prevent concurrent queue dispatch and add scheme/ledger
+   reconciliation and actionable exception alerts.
+3. Remove full payee account identifiers from API responses and complete the notification/status mapping.
+4. Add bank-holiday-aware queueing, atomic balance/daily-limit reservations, and the ledger-backed closure approval check.
+5. Establish statement generation/completeness controls and production database, scheduler, integration, and audit
+   observability before relying on the service for regulatory and operational deadlines.
+
